Both remaining failures are projection-width errors, not filter/join errors: sp0362 returns `*` where gold selects one named column (`Other_Details`, all NULL), and sp0755 returns only the entity table's columns where gold keeps every joined table's columns. The current prompt has a blanket "whole entity -> SELECT T1.*" rule that directly causes sp0755, and nothing tells the model that an attribute word in the question ("details") can name an actual column. So I replace that heuristic with explicit projection rules (attribute-naming words resolved against schema columns first; multi-table joins keep all tables' columns unless attributes are named) and add a deterministic width check in solve.py that spends the second call when the draft's column count looks inconsistent with those rules, showing the model the actual output columns plus the full column lists of the tables it used.

